    "updated_at": "2021-01-06T19:52:14Z",
    "body": "Hey @cardin, sorry for missing this.\r\n\r\nI wrote a quick test script in Python that simply prints the yellow color background a number of times specified by the first argument to the script:\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport sys\r\n\r\nprint(\"\\u001b[43m\",end=\"\")\r\nfor _ in range(int(sys.argv[1])):\r\n    print(\" \", end=\"\")\r\nprint(\"\\u001b[0m\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere's the result when running on windows terminal:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/4605384/103813144-39e12300-5025-11eb-9860-4badfe4d679e.png)\r\n\r\nAs you can see, the yellow color creeps closer to the edge, then when it should only be a few characters over the edge, it fills up two lines. Since I did this with bash, python, and WSL2, instead of powershell/starship/native win10, the only thing our setups share in common is Windows Terminal, which makes me suspect it's a Windows Terminal bug.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, I'm unable to locate an issue that matches *exactly* upstream. It looks like it might be related to [#32](https://github.com/microsoft/terminal/issues/32) there? (It's not an exact match because that appears to only happen when resizing). Either way, I'm not sure if there's a workaround for our end, especially since starship is terminal-agnostic at the moment.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
